/// Block store (`blockstore`, issue 300-STO-0002, docs/storage): immutable blocks named by their content identifier
/// (CIDv1, `raw` or `dag-cbor`, SHA-256; 36 bytes in binary form). Nothing stored is overwritten, and a block is checked against its
/// CID before it is returned: a block whose bytes do not match is reported corrupt, never returned (MC-4.2, 4.8).
/// What a client may do comes from its capability's badge (300-STO-0004); a CID alone grants nothing (MC-4.7).
/// A name's current version points at the root of an object and changes only by compare-and-swap (302-STO-0001).
/// What no name retains and no lease protects is collected (303-STO-0001, 1.1). A name keeps its last 4 versions; it can be
/// removed; an owner (the badge) pins objects; what an owner retains is bounded by its quota (303-STO-0002..0004, 1.2).
/// Several names change at once, all or none, and are read at one point (304-STO-0007, 1.3). The current names are
/// listed by prefix, page by page (300-STO-0018, 1.4). A write says how far it reached, and 1.x is refused (307-STO-0019, 2.0).
/// The records are sealed on the medium, and a volume key that does not open the superblock's check is refused (308-STO-0021, 2.1).
/// Memory domains, each with a key of its own, worked in through a client of their own and forgotten by destroying the key
/// (308-STO-0023, 2.2).
package mind:blockstore@2.2.0;

interface blockstore {
    /// Why a request failed. `rights`: the badge of the client's capability does not allow the request
    /// (`mind::blockstore`: put and collect need BADGE_PUT; get, has, resolve, history, snapshot and names BADGE_GET;
    /// publish, unpublish, commit, pin and unpin BADGE_PUBLISH; stat, pins and usage any).
    /// `invalid`: a `dag-cbor` block that is not a node of `mind::dag` (301-STO-0002), a name that is not 1 to 64 bytes
    /// of `A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ / -`, a root whose tree is out of shape. `conflict`: the name's current version is not the
    /// expected one. `incomplete`: a block of the root's object is not stored. Neither publishes anything. `quota`: the
    /// owner would retain more than its quota; nothing is published or pinned (1.2). `key`: the store is not mounted,
    /// since its volume key does not open the superblock's check or comes from a source this build does not know; every
    /// request gets it (2.1). `no-random`: no random source for a domain's key, which must not be derivable from anything
    /// kept, or it could not be destroyed (2.2).
    enum error { not-found, corrupt, full, too-large, read-only, device, unsupported, rights, invalid, conflict, incomplete, quota, key, no-random }

    /// What a block's bytes are: opaque (`raw`), or a node of an object (`dag-cbor`, `mind::dag`), checked before it is
    /// stored.
    enum codec { raw, dag-cbor }

    /// How far a write reached before its reply (MC-4.4): `volatile`, the store's medium is a RAM disk and nothing
    /// outlives a reset; `flushed`, the medium's driver acknowledged a flush after the write (what a flush reaches is the
    /// driver's to state, docs/storage); `copied`, two media flushed (309, not reached yet) (2.0).
    enum durability { volatile, flushed, copied }

    /// Intact blocks and their bytes; sectors of the log and of the medium; records found corrupt and non-blank
    /// sectors outside every record; how many blocks the index can hold; names published; how far every write reaches
    /// on this medium (2.0).
    record stats { blocks: u32, bytes: u64, used: u64, sectors: u64, corrupt: u32, damaged: u64, capacity: u32, names: u32, durability: durability }

    /// A name's current version (from 1) and the CID of the root it points at (36 bytes).
    record head { version: u64, root: list<u8, 36> }

    /// What a collection freed: block records (unretained, duplicate or corrupt copies), superseded name records, their
    /// sectors; and the blank sectors after it (1.1).
    record collected { blocks: u32, names: u32, sectors: u64, free: u64 }

    /// A version a name keeps: its number and root; no root: the version removed the name (1.2).
    record kept { version: u64, root: list<u8, 36> }

    /// A pin of the caller's owner: its id, the object's root and size (1.2).
    record pinned { id: u32, root: list<u8, 36>, size: u64 }

    /// What the caller's owner (the badge of its capability) retains, counting each root once, its quota, and its
    /// names and pins (1.2).
    record usage { retained: u64, quota: u64, names: u32, pins: u32 }

    /// One name's change in a commit: the version expected (0: a new name) and the new root; no root removes the
    /// name (1.3).
    record update { name: string<64>, expected: u64, root: list<u8, 36> }

    /// A current name, its version and the root it points at (1.4).
    record named { name: string<64>, version: u64, root: list<u8, 36> }

    /// A publication or a removal: the name's new version, and how far it reached (2.0).
    record published { version: u64, durability: durability }

    /// A pin: its id, and how far it reached (2.0).
    record pin-taken { id: u32, durability: durability }

    /// A commit: the names' new versions, in order, and how far they reached (2.0).
    record committed { versions: list<u64, 8>, durability: durability }

    /// A memory domain of the caller's owner: its number, and whether it is forgotten, until a collection has freed its
    /// records (2.2).
    record domain { id: u16, forgotten: bool }

    /// Stores `data` as a block of type `codec` and returns its CID once the medium has flushed it; a block already
    /// stored is not written again. How far it reached is the medium's, in `stat`: a put is retained only by its lease
    /// until a name or a pin retains its object (MC-4.11). Errors: invalid (not a node), too-large, full (medium or
    /// index), read-only, device.
    put: func(codec: codec, data: bytes<16384>) -> result<bytes<36>, error>;
    /// The block `cid` names, only if its bytes match it. Errors: unsupported (not a supported CID), not-found,
    /// corrupt (the block also leaves the index, so a put of the same bytes stores them again), device.
    get: func(cid: bytes<36>) -> result<bytes<16384>, error>;
    /// Whether the index holds `cid`; the block is not read. Error: unsupported.
    has: func(cid: bytes<36>) -> result<bool, error>;
    stat: func() -> result<stats, error>;
    /// Makes `root` the next version of `name` if `expected` is its current version (0: a new name), once every block
    /// of the object `root` names is stored; returns the new version after the medium has flushed it (MC-4.3, 4.4).
    /// Errors: conflict, incomplete, invalid, unsupported, corrupt, full, read-only, device.
    publish: func(name: string<64>, expected: u64, root: bytes<36>) -> result<published, error>;
    /// The current version of `name` and its root. Errors: not-found, invalid.
    resolve: func(name: string<64>) -> result<head, error>;
    /// Frees what no name's current version retains and no lease protects (a block is leased for 60 s after its last
    /// put, and after the store mounts). Every name's object is walked first; if one lacks a block or holds a corrupt
    /// node, nothing is freed (incomplete, corrupt). A put that finds no room runs one too (1.1).
    collect: func() -> result<collected, error>;
    /// Removes `name` if `expected` is its current version: a version without a root, which ends what the name retains
    /// (the objects go when nothing else retains them). Returns that version; publishing from it creates the name again.
    /// Errors: not-found (no such name, or removed), conflict, full, read-only, device (1.2).
    unpublish: func(name: string<64>, expected: u64) -> result<published, error>;
    /// The versions `name` keeps, newest first: the current one and up to 3 before it, each retained (1.2).
    history: func(name: string<64>) -> result<list<kept, 4>, error>;
    /// Pins the object `root` for the caller's owner until it unpins it, once every block of it is stored and within
    /// the owner's quota; returns the pin's id. Errors: incomplete, quota, full, invalid, unsupported, read-only (1.2).
    pin: func(root: bytes<36>) -> result<pin-taken, error>;
    /// Ends a pin of the caller's owner. Errors: not-found, rights (another owner's pin) (1.2).
    unpin: func(id: u32) -> result<_, error>;
    /// The pins of the caller's owner, at most 32 (1.2).
    pins: func() -> result<list<pinned, 32>, error>;
    /// What the caller's owner retains and may retain (1.2).
    usage: func() -> result<usage, error>;
    /// Changes up to 8 names at once, all or none (MC-4.10): each from the version it expects, to a complete root or
    /// to a removal, within the caller's quota. One record with one digest holds every change, flushed before the
    /// reply; requests are served one at a time, so no reader sees part of a commit. Returns the new versions, in
    /// order. Errors: invalid (a name twice, or 0 or more than 8), conflict, incomplete, not-found (removing what is
    /// not there), quota, full, read-only, device; each changes nothing (1.3).
    commit: func(updates: list<update, 8>) -> result<committed, error>;
    /// The current version and root of up to 8 names, read at one point between commits: version 0 and no root for
    /// a name that does not exist, no root for a removed one (1.3).
    snapshot: func(names: list<string<64>, 8>) -> result<list<head, 8>, error>;
    /// Up to 16 current names that start with `prefix`, in byte order, each after `after` (empty: from the first); a
    /// removed name is not listed. The next page is asked for after the last name of this one, until one comes back
    /// with fewer than 16. Knowing a name grants nothing: reading its object needs the right still (MC-4.7). Error:
    /// invalid (`prefix` or `after` holds a byte no name has) (1.4).
    names: func(prefix: string<64>, after: string<64>) -> result<list<named, 16>, error>;

    /// Makes a memory domain for the caller's owner, with a key of its own, random, kept in a record sealed under the
    /// volume key (308-STO-0023); returns its number. Every other call works in the volume's own domain, or in the
    /// domain of the client it comes through. Errors: no-random, full (32 domains, live and forgotten), read-only,
    /// device; rights through a domain's client (2.2).
    domain-create: func() -> result<u16, error>;
    /// A client whose every call works in domain `id` of the caller's owner, with the caller's rights: the domain's
    /// names, pins and blocks, sealed under its key. The same bytes in two domains are two blocks. The client ends when
    /// the domain is forgotten or the store restarts. Errors: not-found (no such live domain), rights (another owner's
    /// domain, or asked through a domain's client), full (2.2).
    domain-client: func(id: u16) -> result<borrow<endpoint>, error>;
    /// Forgets domain `id` of the caller's owner (MC-4.8): a tombstone is written, then its key record erased. Its
    /// names, pins and blocks are gone at once and its clients revoked; its records never open again, and the next
    /// collection frees them. Copies already read, and a flash medium's spare blocks, are outside this. Errors:
    /// not-found, rights, full, read-only, device (2.2).
    domain-forget: func(id: u16) -> result<_, error>;
    /// The domains of the caller's owner (2.2).
    domains: func() -> result<list<domain, 32>, error>;
}
